## The problem

The green dot lies, and you've been on the receiving end of both ways it lies.

One: you have the app open on your phone and a tab open on your laptop. You close the tab. Your teammates watch you go offline while you're still holding the phone, mid-conversation. Two: the server restarts during a Tuesday deploy, and everyone who was online is now online forever — the `users.is_online` column still says `true`, and nothing will ever set it back, because the process that would have done it no longer exists.

Third symptom, same root cause: "Aswin is typing…" pinned under a conversation for six hours. All three come from storing momentary, derived state as if it were a durable fact.

## The idea

Two kinds of state.

**Facts.** A message was sent. Someone said something at a time, that's still true tomorrow, and you can be asked about it in a support ticket. Facts go to Postgres.

**Weather.** Aswin is online. Aswin is typing. True for a few seconds, meaningless afterwards, and *derived* from something else — whether a socket is open right now. Weather lives in memory and should evaporate when the process does.

`is_online boolean` is a fact-shaped hole you pour weather into. It fails for exactly the reason a `useState` mirror of a prop fails in React: two sources for one truth, drifting the moment something unusual happens — a second device, a crash, a tunnel. The React fix is to derive instead of copy, and the fix here is identical: online is *computed* from live connections, never written down.

## In practice

Online, as a reference count instead of a boolean. Keyed by user, values are the sets of that user's live sockets:

```ts
// userId → the set of sockets this user currently has open.
// Two devices means two entries and one person.
const onlineUsers = new Map();

export function userConnected(userId: string, ws: WebSocket) {
  if (!onlineUsers.has(userId)) {
    onlineUsers.set(userId, new Set());
    broadcastPresence(userId, "online"); // first connection only
  }
  onlineUsers.get(userId).add(ws);
}

export function userDisconnected(userId: string, ws: WebSocket) {
  const sockets = onlineUsers.get(userId);
  if (!sockets) return;

  sockets.delete(ws);
  if (sockets.size === 0) {
    // last connection only
    onlineUsers.delete(userId);
    clearTypingFor(userId);
    broadcastPresence(userId, "offline");
  }
}
```

Read that twice. Both broadcasts sit inside a condition, and those two conditions *are* the feature: announce on the first connect and the last disconnect, nothing in between. Delete the `size === 0` check and closing a laptop tab marks you offline on your phone — the opening bug, in one line.

Typing, with a TTL. The state is a timer, not a flag:

```ts
const TYPING_TTL_MS = 3000;

// channelId → (userId → the timer that will clear them)
const typingByChannel = new Map();

export function startTyping(userId: string, channelId: string) {
  const inChannel = typingByChannel.get(channelId) ?? new Map();
  typingByChannel.set(channelId, inChannel);

  const existing = inChannel.get(userId);
  if (existing) clearTimeout(existing);
  else broadcast(channelId, { type: "user_typing", user_id: userId });

  inChannel.set(
    userId,
    setTimeout(() => {
      inChannel.delete(userId);
      broadcast(channelId, { type: "user_stop_typing", user_id: userId });
    }, TYPING_TTL_MS),
  );
}
```

Two design choices worth naming. The client **debounces** — it sends `start_typing` at most once a second, because a fast typist otherwise generates ten events a second for a UI that needs one. And the server relies on a **TTL**, not an explicit stop. Users don't stop typing; they trail off, background the app, or lose signal. A `stop_typing` frame that never arrives leaves the indicator stuck forever; a timer makes the worst case a three-second-late clear.

Now rate limits, because you've just built something with no cost per message. A script with a valid token and a `while` loop can push a thousand `POST /messages` per second: a thousand rows written, a thousand events fanned out, a thousand frames delivered to every client in the channel. One user, everyone's app janking.

**Token bucket.** Each user gets a bucket of capacity N, each request costs one token, tokens refill at R per second, and an empty bucket means 429:

```ts
interface Bucket {
  tokens: number;
  lastRefill: number;
}

const buckets = new Map();

export const rateLimit =
  (capacity: number, refillPerSec: number) => (req, res, next) => {
    const key = req.user?.id; // user id, not IP
    if (!key) return next();

    const now = Date.now();
    const b = buckets.get(key) ?? { tokens: capacity, lastRefill: now };
    const elapsedSec = (now - b.lastRefill) / 1000;
    b.tokens = Math.min(capacity, b.tokens + elapsedSec * refillPerSec);
    b.lastRefill = now;

    if (b.tokens < 1) {
      const retryAfter = Math.ceil((1 - b.tokens) / refillPerSec);
      res.setHeader("Retry-After", retryAfter);
      return res
        .status(429)
        .json({ error: "Too many requests", retry_after: retryAfter });
    }

    b.tokens -= 1;
    buckets.set(key, b);
    next();
  };
```

Why a bucket and not a counter? "Sixty per minute" lets someone fire sixty messages in the first second and then blocks them for fifty-nine — spiky for the server, baffling for the user. Capacity 20 with a refill of 2 per second lets a real person catch up in a fast conversation while holding the long-run average at two per second. Burst *and* ceiling, in two numbers.

Where the bucket lives is a real decision. In memory it's a `Map` and zero infrastructure — and it resets on every deploy, and with three instances behind a load balancer each user gets three buckets, so your "20 per second" is really 60. Redis makes it shared and restart-proof, at the cost of a network hop per request. Start in memory; know exactly what you traded.

## In your world

`chat-app-api` has all of this: `src/services/presence.service.ts` holds the two maps, and `src/middleware/rate-limiter.ts` holds the bucket plus a cleanup interval that evicts idle users. Read that interval and work out what happens without it after a month of traffic — every user who ever sent one message, still resident.

Then apply it where it counts. Your own standards say auth endpoints get rate limits, and this is why: login and password reset are where an attacker's cost matters most. Note the awkward part — a login has no `req.user` yet, so you're keying on IP plus the submitted identifier, with all the shared-NAT unfairness that implies. Presence escalates the same way: your social app's "active now" is this reference-count problem at a scale where in-memory stops working, which is where Redis pub/sub or Supabase's CRDT-based Presence earns its place.

## Watch out

- **A boolean column cannot represent multiple devices.** If you must persist something, persist a `last_seen_at` timestamp you overwrite — and still treat "online" as derived from live connections.
- **Clear typing state on disconnect.** A user who drops mid-word otherwise leaves a timer firing into a channel they've left, or an indicator that never clears. Call it from the last-disconnect branch, not only from `stop_typing`.
- **Key limits on user id once you have an authenticated user.** An office or a mobile carrier NAT shares one IP across hundreds of people.
- **Every map in this module is a potential leak.** Socket sets shrink on disconnect, timers clear themselves, buckets need a sweeper. Write the eviction story at the same time as the feature.
- **A deploy is a free-limit window.** In-memory buckets reset to full. If that matters for the endpoint — and for auth it does — the bucket belongs in Redis.

